    {"id": 1, "fact": "In City of Ladue v. Gilleo (1994), the Supreme Court struck down a municipal ordinance that completely banned off-premises signs, holding that such a total ban violates the First Amendment because it forecloses a unique and important medium of expression.", "source_date": "1994-06-27"},
    {"id": 2, "fact": "In City of Los Angeles v. Preferred Communications, Inc. (1986), the Supreme Court held that a municipality's grant of an exclusive franchise to a cable operator does not automatically immunize the municipality from First Amendment scrutiny regarding the regulation of speech.", "source_date": "1986-06-25"},
    {"id": 3, "fact": "In Metromedia, Inc. v. City of San Diego (1981), the Supreme Court ruled that a municipal ordinance banning off-premises commercial signs while allowing on-premises signs was unconstitutional because it discriminated based on the content of the speech, violating the First Amendment.", "source_date": "1981-06-23"},
    {"id": 4, "fact": "In Reed v. Town of Gilbert (2015), the Supreme Court established that laws regulating speech based on content are subject to strict scrutiny, requiring the government to prove the law is narrowly tailored to serve a compelling state interest.", "source_date": "2015-06-18"},
